        "aiSupplement": {
          "explanation": [
            {
              "text": "LiAlH<sub>4</sub> delivers hydride ions (H<sup>–</sup>), which attack electron-poor carbon atoms such as the δ+ carbon of a C=O group. It reduces carboxylic acids to primary alcohols: CH<sub>3</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>H + 4[H] → CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH + H<sub>2</sub>O.",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:18.1(2)"
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "A C=C bond is electron-rich and non-polar, so hydride ions do not attack it. Alkenes are hydrogenated with H<sub>2</sub>(g) and a Pt or Ni catalyst instead.",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:14.2(2)"
              ]
            }
          ],
